WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.020 --> 00:00:01.853
- Hey, good humans.

00:00:01.853 --> 00:00:02.686
Milky here.

00:00:02.686 --> 00:00:03.600
- And Timmy.

00:00:03.600 --> 00:00:06.390
- Timmy, what would you
do if you won the lottery?

00:00:06.390 --> 00:00:09.600
- Hmm. Maybe long walks on the beach.

00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:11.340
All day, every day.

00:00:11.340 --> 00:00:12.240
But you know what?

00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:14.100
I actually hate the wet sand.

00:00:14.100 --> 00:00:16.100
- Oh, have you heard of
that sneaker brand...

00:00:16.100 --> 00:00:18.180
Vessi?

00:00:18.180 --> 00:00:19.230
I've struggled with not knowing

00:00:19.230 --> 00:00:21.000
what shoes to wear during the wet season.

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:22.110
I'm tired of boots

00:00:22.110 --> 00:00:24.210
and I don't want anything
too bulky or heavy.

00:00:24.210 --> 00:00:25.650
So I love my Vessis because

00:00:25.650 --> 00:00:28.500
they're extremely lightweight,
comfortable, and breathable

00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:30.120
- [Timmy] And they come in stylish designs

00:00:30.120 --> 00:00:32.310
for an everyday sneaker of any season.

00:00:32.310 --> 00:00:34.860
- [Milky] I love that
Vessis are a 100% waterproof

00:00:34.860 --> 00:00:36.450
and great for unpredictable weather.

00:00:36.450 --> 00:00:38.160
Especially being in LA.

00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:39.390
I have them right by my front door

00:00:39.390 --> 00:00:41.430
and slip them on without a second thought.

00:00:41.430 --> 00:00:42.720
- [Timmy] They're made from Dyma-tex,

00:00:42.720 --> 00:00:44.100
a dual climate knit material

00:00:44.100 --> 00:00:45.450
that keeps you cool in the summer

00:00:45.450 --> 00:00:46.980
and warm in colder weather.

00:00:46.980 --> 00:00:48.112
It doesn't feel like it
should be waterproof,

00:00:48.112 --> 00:00:49.440
but it is

00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:51.240
- [Milk] They're so cute and effortless

00:00:51.240 --> 00:00:54.150
and have saved my feet from soggy socks.

00:00:54.150 --> 00:00:56.310
- [Timmy] Vessis are the
perfect gift under the tree.

00:00:56.310 --> 00:00:57.270
And for your feet.

00:00:57.270 --> 00:01:00.990
Check out their holiday
sale at vessi.com/jubilee.

00:01:00.990 --> 00:01:03.900
Get the style and size you
want now before they sell out.

00:01:03.900 --> 00:01:05.100
If you missed out on the sale

00:01:05.100 --> 00:01:08.310
use code Jubilee for 15%
off your entire order.

00:01:08.310 --> 00:01:10.080
- Thanks, Vessi, for
sponsoring this video.

00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:12.480
Now please enjoy the episode.

00:01:12.480 --> 00:01:14.249
What did your scream sound like?

00:01:14.249 --> 00:01:15.307
- It was like, ah!

00:01:17.021 --> 00:01:17.854
I don't know.

00:01:17.854 --> 00:01:20.582
That's, that's, I'm not doing it justice.

00:01:20.582 --> 00:01:21.415
- Ah!

00:01:21.415 --> 00:01:22.248
- It wasn't that, it wasn't that.

00:01:22.248 --> 00:01:23.081
- [Milky] If you were alone in a room

00:01:23.081 --> 00:01:24.930
with someone who won the lottery,

00:01:24.930 --> 00:01:26.310
what would you ask?

00:01:26.310 --> 00:01:28.560
- What was the first thing you bought

00:01:28.560 --> 00:01:29.430
when you won the lottery?

00:01:29.430 --> 00:01:31.530
- Have you felt taken advantage

00:01:31.530 --> 00:01:33.660
of by friends or relatives?

00:01:33.660 --> 00:01:35.970
- Do you think that winning
the lottery has like

00:01:35.970 --> 00:01:39.870
exponentially like
added to your happiness?

00:01:39.870 --> 00:01:41.270
- I think that...

00:01:44.760 --> 00:01:45.593
- Hi, I'm Andrew.

00:01:45.593 --> 00:01:46.440
Nice to meet you.

00:01:46.440 --> 00:01:47.273
- Hi, Andrew.

00:01:47.273 --> 00:01:48.750
I'm, I'm Timothy.

00:01:48.750 --> 00:01:49.583
Nice to meet you.

00:01:49.583 --> 00:01:50.416
- Yeah, what's up?

00:01:50.416 --> 00:01:51.450
Big balling boy.

00:01:51.450 --> 00:01:52.860
What's cracking?

00:01:52.860 --> 00:01:53.820
- Hey, how you doing?

00:01:53.820 --> 00:01:56.160
- Dude, I'm doing good, bro.

00:01:56.160 --> 00:01:58.860
It's, dude, it, how much was it, man?

00:01:58.860 --> 00:01:59.693
We wanna know.

00:01:59.693 --> 00:02:00.720
Or I wanna know.

00:02:00.720 --> 00:02:04.140
- Oh. When I won, it was 28 million.

00:02:04.140 --> 00:02:05.490
- Jesus!

00:02:05.490 --> 00:02:06.323
28 million?

00:02:06.323 --> 00:02:08.412
If I had 28 million right now, you man.

00:02:08.412 --> 00:02:11.400
Ooh, I'd go to Cheesecake Factory.

00:02:11.400 --> 00:02:12.810
- So when did you win the lottery?

00:02:12.810 --> 00:02:13.890
And how old were you?

00:02:13.890 --> 00:02:18.150
- I won in 1999, and I was 21 years old.

00:02:18.150 --> 00:02:18.983
- Oh, wow.

00:02:18.983 --> 00:02:22.470
How did you come into this
like, winning lottery ticket?

00:02:22.470 --> 00:02:25.380
- Worked at a gas station
48 to 60 hours a week

00:02:25.380 --> 00:02:27.390
for a little over minimum wage

00:02:27.390 --> 00:02:29.910
and I sold it to myself
while I was working.

00:02:29.910 --> 00:02:33.690
- Were you somebody who
played the lottery frequently?

00:02:33.690 --> 00:02:36.000
Like was this your dream
to win the lottery?

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:39.060
- Kind of a crazy story,
but I actually had a dream

00:02:39.060 --> 00:02:40.980
about winning the lottery a few months

00:02:40.980 --> 00:02:43.290
before it happened in real life.

00:02:43.290 --> 00:02:46.320
So I, I was living in an efficiency,

00:02:46.320 --> 00:02:48.060
sleeping on an air mattress,

00:02:48.060 --> 00:02:49.890
just trying to put myself through college.

00:02:49.890 --> 00:02:53.430
And I had this very vivid dream
that I had won the lottery.

00:02:53.430 --> 00:02:56.880
I woke up and it took a
little bit to understand

00:02:56.880 --> 00:02:58.740
that it hadn't already happened.

00:02:58.740 --> 00:02:59.847
It was was it was that real.

00:02:59.847 --> 00:03:02.640
And so I, after that point in time,

00:03:02.640 --> 00:03:03.720
I started playing the lottery

00:03:03.720 --> 00:03:05.670
a little more consistently
because I believed

00:03:05.670 --> 00:03:07.860
that it, you know, it could happen

00:03:07.860 --> 00:03:09.300
or that it was likely to happen.

00:03:09.300 --> 00:03:11.640
And some people think
that's just coincidence

00:03:11.640 --> 00:03:13.910
but you know, it, it happened.

00:03:13.910 --> 00:03:15.960
- So what was it like winning it?

00:03:15.960 --> 00:03:18.540
- Oh, it was so surreal.

00:03:18.540 --> 00:03:19.679
- [Interviewer] Right.

00:03:19.679 --> 00:03:20.808
- I mean, it's one of the,

00:03:20.808 --> 00:03:21.693
the craziest things

00:03:21.693 --> 00:03:22.526
that that's ever happened to me.

00:03:22.526 --> 00:03:25.410
I mean, it was, it was
one minute you're working

00:03:25.410 --> 00:03:26.793
at a gas station,

00:03:26.793 --> 00:03:27.626
- Right.

00:03:27.626 --> 00:03:28.890
- Next minute you win millions of dollars.

00:03:28.890 --> 00:03:31.590
I mean, your life is turned on its head.

00:03:31.590 --> 00:03:32.970
It was crazy.

00:03:32.970 --> 00:03:35.760
- Could you recreate that, that energy?

00:03:35.760 --> 00:03:36.660
Can you, like, how,

00:03:36.660 --> 00:03:38.130
what did your scream sound like?

00:03:38.130 --> 00:03:38.963
- Ah!

00:03:40.320 --> 00:03:41.670
I don't know.

00:03:41.670 --> 00:03:44.382
That's, that's, I'm not doing it justice.

00:03:44.382 --> 00:03:45.215
- Ah!

00:03:45.215 --> 00:03:46.048
- It wasn't that, it wasn't that.

00:03:46.048 --> 00:03:47.670
- What was the first thing you bought

00:03:47.670 --> 00:03:48.540
when you won the lottery?

00:03:48.540 --> 00:03:50.220
Like, first thing, even if it was gas.

00:03:50.220 --> 00:03:51.900
Just what was the first
transaction you made

00:03:51.900 --> 00:03:53.100
after you won the lottery?

00:03:53.100 --> 00:03:54.420
- This is gonna sound kind of funny

00:03:54.420 --> 00:03:57.150
but it was actually a video game system.

00:03:57.150 --> 00:03:59.340
It was the latest,
greatest video game system.

00:03:59.340 --> 00:04:02.280
It was a N64, and I'm dating myself

00:04:02.280 --> 00:04:04.110
because that's ancient.

00:04:04.110 --> 00:04:05.760
But I was 21 years old.

00:04:05.760 --> 00:04:08.430
This was 1999, and that thing

00:04:08.430 --> 00:04:10.080
at the time was awesome.

00:04:10.080 --> 00:04:12.300
And so that's the first thing I purchased.

00:04:12.300 --> 00:04:13.920
- What's the most expensive
thing that you've bought?

00:04:13.920 --> 00:04:15.210
- I've never really been

00:04:15.210 --> 00:04:17.640
into material possessions, too much.

00:04:17.640 --> 00:04:20.550
I've purchased some real
estate, new vehicles.

00:04:20.550 --> 00:04:22.410
The nicest car I've ever purchased

00:04:22.410 --> 00:04:26.610
was probably a new Audi A4.

00:04:26.610 --> 00:04:27.795
- After you get that money,

00:04:27.795 --> 00:04:28.770
I feel like you have some kind

00:04:28.770 --> 00:04:30.450
of clarity from certain anxieties

00:04:30.450 --> 00:04:31.890
like you don't think about,

00:04:31.890 --> 00:04:33.720
you know the bills you gotta pay.

00:04:33.720 --> 00:04:36.000
What was the thing you
had to worry about after

00:04:36.000 --> 00:04:38.850
you got like, that anxiety
covered of having to survive?

00:04:38.850 --> 00:04:40.650
What was like the next anxiety?

00:04:40.650 --> 00:04:42.240
- The next one was making sure

00:04:42.240 --> 00:04:44.070
that people didn't want to use me.

00:04:44.070 --> 00:04:44.903
- [Interviewer] Right.

00:04:44.903 --> 00:04:45.750
- In trusting people.

00:04:45.750 --> 00:04:47.940
I mean, that was, that
was the next big anxiety.

00:04:47.940 --> 00:04:52.170
- Have you felt taken advantage
of by friends or relatives?

00:04:52.170 --> 00:04:53.970
- You know, people can
come out of the woodwork,

00:04:53.970 --> 00:04:55.860
especially if you have to
claim the prize publicly

00:04:55.860 --> 00:04:57.000
or if you choose to.

00:04:57.000 --> 00:04:58.260
That's, that's very real.

00:04:58.260 --> 00:04:59.160
That happened to me.

00:04:59.160 --> 00:05:00.990
I couldn't even go into a grocery store

00:05:00.990 --> 00:05:02.490
without people knowing who I was.

00:05:02.490 --> 00:05:03.330
- Dang.

00:05:03.330 --> 00:05:04.170
- So I had to move.

00:05:04.170 --> 00:05:07.770
There were people, they wanted
me to pay for their debt.

00:05:07.770 --> 00:05:12.570
I received stacks of
letters from people asking

00:05:12.570 --> 00:05:15.060
pleading for money, and
of course I felt sorry

00:05:15.060 --> 00:05:17.300
for, for everyone that
has asked for money.

00:05:17.300 --> 00:05:19.770
I, I did a lot for other people.

00:05:19.770 --> 00:05:21.233
I did everything that I could

00:05:21.233 --> 00:05:24.210
but I also had an understanding
that I couldn't just give

00:05:24.210 --> 00:05:25.500
and give and give everything or,

00:05:25.500 --> 00:05:27.630
you know I would go broke rather quickly

00:05:27.630 --> 00:05:29.730
because there's too much
disparity in the world.

00:05:29.730 --> 00:05:32.460
They view it as you get something

00:05:32.460 --> 00:05:36.420
for nothing and therefore
I should be entitled to it.

00:05:36.420 --> 00:05:38.010
Some people feel that way.

00:05:38.010 --> 00:05:40.360
- How sexy is your credit score right now?

00:05:40.360 --> 00:05:42.120
- I, I mean, I can't complain.

00:05:42.120 --> 00:05:43.290
I can't complain about my--

00:05:43.290 --> 00:05:44.430
- So, it's pretty sexy.

00:05:44.430 --> 00:05:45.330
It's pretty sexy

00:05:45.330 --> 00:05:46.650
- I would say so.

00:05:46.650 --> 00:05:48.820
- Like, Sports Illustrated cover sexy.

00:05:48.820 --> 00:05:49.653
- Yeah, Yeah.

00:05:49.653 --> 00:05:51.990
Walking down the, the runway

00:05:51.990 --> 00:05:52.823
- How do you feel about like,

00:05:52.823 --> 00:05:54.630
the taxes in the US for example?

00:05:54.630 --> 00:05:58.170
Like should you know
people who have your amount

00:05:58.170 --> 00:05:59.430
of wealth be taxed?

00:05:59.430 --> 00:06:01.140
- Well, first of all,
if you win the lottery

00:06:01.140 --> 00:06:02.580
in the United States, that is,

00:06:02.580 --> 00:06:04.350
the initial payment when you go to

00:06:04.350 --> 00:06:06.030
the lottery office and claim the prize

00:06:06.030 --> 00:06:08.760
the initial payment that
you receive is heavily

00:06:08.760 --> 00:06:10.920
heavily taxed because they, you know,

00:06:10.920 --> 00:06:12.420
it's part of income tax

00:06:12.420 --> 00:06:15.690
and it's very, very high
in the United States.

00:06:15.690 --> 00:06:17.280
And so there are lots

00:06:17.280 --> 00:06:21.840
of people that argue that
it should not be so high.

00:06:21.840 --> 00:06:23.190
- How much do you have left?

00:06:23.190 --> 00:06:25.590
- Well, I can't publicly say that

00:06:25.590 --> 00:06:28.140
because of a non-disclosure agreement.

00:06:28.140 --> 00:06:29.370
Non-disclosure agreement.

00:06:29.370 --> 00:06:30.420
Non-disclosure agreement,

00:06:30.420 --> 00:06:32.880
which prevents me from talking about that.

00:06:32.880 --> 00:06:34.440
- What do you do for a living now?

00:06:34.440 --> 00:06:37.740
- I direct a podcast that I created

00:06:37.740 --> 00:06:40.680
on, on YouTube where I'm
interviewing other people

00:06:40.680 --> 00:06:41.580
that I find fascinating

00:06:41.580 --> 00:06:43.560
including some other lottery winners

00:06:43.560 --> 00:06:46.050
or people that have
overcome the odds in life.

00:06:46.050 --> 00:06:49.650
It's, it's more of a labor
of love, I would say.

00:06:49.650 --> 00:06:50.970
- So you don't need to be working.

00:06:50.970 --> 00:06:51.803
- No.

00:06:51.803 --> 00:06:52.860
- Why don't you retire now?

00:06:52.860 --> 00:06:56.490
- You, you can argue that I
retired when I was 21 years old.

00:06:56.490 --> 00:06:58.200
You know, it took me
a while to understand,

00:06:58.200 --> 00:07:01.440
to try to understand
what, what makes me happy.

00:07:01.440 --> 00:07:03.570
And I think for a lot of people,

00:07:03.570 --> 00:07:05.139
might feel this way, like when you wake up

00:07:05.139 --> 00:07:06.870
in the morning, are you looking forward

00:07:06.870 --> 00:07:07.860
to something?

00:07:07.860 --> 00:07:10.044
I couldn't just sit on
a beach every day for,

00:07:10.044 --> 00:07:12.604
forever and just, you know,

00:07:12.604 --> 00:07:16.110
drink mai tais and Coronas or whatever

00:07:16.110 --> 00:07:18.450
or, you know, just be a beach bum.

00:07:18.450 --> 00:07:19.770
Some people maybe they could,

00:07:19.770 --> 00:07:21.840
but for me I needed a purpose.

00:07:21.840 --> 00:07:23.130
I needed a reason to wake up

00:07:23.130 --> 00:07:24.810
in the morning and look forward to life.

00:07:24.810 --> 00:07:27.510
- Do you think that winning
the lottery has like

00:07:27.510 --> 00:07:30.810
exponentially like
added to your happiness?

00:07:30.810 --> 00:07:33.319
- I think that, I mean, it's allowed

00:07:33.319 --> 00:07:36.300
in the sense that it's
allowed me to pursue my dreams

00:07:36.300 --> 00:07:40.530
without stressing so much
about financial obligations.

00:07:40.530 --> 00:07:41.430
It has.

00:07:41.430 --> 00:07:42.263
- Yeah.

00:07:42.263 --> 00:07:43.336
That, that makes sense.

00:07:43.336 --> 00:07:44.169
Yeah.

00:07:44.169 --> 00:07:45.002
- But, but it hasn't
changed me as a person.

00:07:45.002 --> 00:07:46.500
I mean, if I had not won the lottery

00:07:46.500 --> 00:07:49.320
I still would've pursued
film and journalism

00:07:49.320 --> 00:07:51.330
and pursuing my dreams,

00:07:51.330 --> 00:07:54.030
because 99.9% of everyone

00:07:54.030 --> 00:07:56.400
that's ever achieved their
dreams has not won the lottery.

00:07:56.400 --> 00:07:58.830
And you absolutely don't have to,

00:07:58.830 --> 00:07:59.663
but it can help.

00:07:59.663 --> 00:08:00.733
- Yeah.

00:08:00.733 --> 00:08:02.100
That is true.

00:08:02.100 --> 00:08:04.603
- Do you feel like you deserve
the money that you won?

00:08:04.603 --> 00:08:05.460
- I do.

00:08:05.460 --> 00:08:06.300
I do.

00:08:06.300 --> 00:08:07.980
I mean, I'm forever grateful though.

00:08:07.980 --> 00:08:10.962
I don't, do I deserve it more than someone

00:08:10.962 --> 00:08:12.860
on the street?

00:08:12.860 --> 00:08:16.050
You know, not necessarily, you know.

00:08:16.050 --> 00:08:19.890
After I, when I initially won, I,

00:08:19.890 --> 00:08:21.240
I mean, the exhilaration is

00:08:21.240 --> 00:08:24.030
through the roof and I was
so unbelievably excited,

00:08:24.030 --> 00:08:28.650
but most of my peers and my
family were not at that place.

00:08:28.650 --> 00:08:31.218
And so once the exhilaration wore off

00:08:31.218 --> 00:08:35.730
I even felt a sense of
guilt for having one.

00:08:35.730 --> 00:08:38.550
And I came across this thing
called Sudden Wealth Syndrome

00:08:38.550 --> 00:08:43.350
which I would have no idea
what the heck that even means.

00:08:43.350 --> 00:08:44.340
I looked it up and I was like

00:08:44.340 --> 00:08:46.110
that is really funny because I feel

00:08:46.110 --> 00:08:49.920
like I'm experiencing a lot
of these types of things.

00:08:49.920 --> 00:08:51.090
- Knowing what you know now

00:08:51.090 --> 00:08:54.810
would you recommend other
people to buy a lottery ticket

00:08:54.810 --> 00:08:57.210
or would you recommend
other ways to make money?

00:08:58.452 --> 00:09:01.140
Well, obviously, you know, it's very...

00:09:01.140 --> 00:09:04.890
- Well, my, my advice,
my advice is to only

00:09:04.890 --> 00:09:05.820
if you play the lottery,

00:09:05.820 --> 00:09:08.160
only spend what you can afford.

00:09:08.160 --> 00:09:09.960
But the risk reward payoff.

00:09:09.960 --> 00:09:10.860
I mean, is huge.

00:09:10.860 --> 00:09:12.810
If you're spending $2 on a ticket

00:09:12.810 --> 00:09:14.610
and you hit millions of dollars, I mean,

00:09:14.610 --> 00:09:15.720
that's a pretty big deal.

00:09:15.720 --> 00:09:18.090
But never spend more than you can afford

00:09:18.090 --> 00:09:19.650
and have fun with it.

00:09:19.650 --> 00:09:21.900
And remember that you
do have a chance to win

00:09:21.900 --> 00:09:24.060
if you play because
that, that is the truth.

00:09:24.060 --> 00:09:26.010
And that's, that's my advice.

00:09:26.010 --> 00:09:27.113
I mean, you can't win if you don't play.

00:09:27.113 --> 00:09:29.625
And I hate to be that guy, but it's true.

00:09:29.625 --> 00:09:30.870
- [Producer] And would you
guys like to meet each other?

00:09:30.870 --> 00:09:31.703
- Sure.

00:09:31.703 --> 00:09:32.550
- I would love to meet Timothy

00:09:32.550 --> 00:09:34.500
after hearing his soothing voice.

00:09:34.500 --> 00:09:35.430
- Hi there.

